## §4 — System Prompt Design

### 4.1 — Template Structure

```
[IDENTITY]       — "You are tau, a coding agent..."
[WORKSPACE]      — CWD, OS, shell, date
[RULES]          — Behavioral rules (don't guess, verify, etc.)
[SKILLS_INDEX]   — Available skills list (from SkillLoader)
[TOOL_DESCS]     — Tool list with schemas (from sess.Tools.Active())
[DYNAMIC]        — Git status, open files, recent changes (optional)
```

### 4.2 — Dynamic Injection

Dynamic context is injected each turn via `TransformContext` hook:

```go
sess.Hooks.TransformContext.Add(func(ctx context.Context, msgs []core.Message) ([]core.Message, error) {
    gitStatus := runGitStatus()
    openFiles := editorTracker.OpenFiles()
    prefix := formatContextBlock(gitStatus, openFiles)
    // prepend as system message at position 0
    return append([]core.Message{{Role: core.RoleSystem, Content: prefix}}, msgs...), nil
})
```

### 4.3 — Prompt File Convention

Prompts stored as `.md` templates in `pkg/coding/prompts/`:

```
prompts/
  system_base.md        — identity + rules
  system_workspace.md   — workspace context template
```

Loaded via `embed.FS`, rendered with `text/template`. User can override via `--system-prompt` flag.

